    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one more thing, regarding the downstream effects—if the ACE inhibitor is effectively 'stopping' the process mid-way, does that lead to an accumulation of the precursors (like angiotensin I) or any other stuff building up in the blood? just trying to visualize the whole flow.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you are absolutely right to consider the precursors; when you block a step, the upstream components tend to accumulate. Since lisinopril is blocking the conversion of Angiotensin I into Angiotensin II, you will see an accumulation of Angiotensin I in the bloodstream. Furthermore, because Angiotensin II normally stimulates the release of Aldosterone, the lack of Angiotensin II can also lead to compensatory increases in other RAAS components, like renin itself, as the body tries to overcome the blockade. This entire upstream buildup is a key feature of ACE inhibitor action.",
      "source": "target",
